  "worked_solution": "Let $u=2-x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}x=-\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=2-u.$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{{(2-x)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x{(2-x)}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{0}(2-u){u}^{3/2}(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{0}(u-2){u}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{0}({u}^{5/2}-2{u}^{3/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{7}{u}^{7/2}-2\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2})|}_{2}^{0} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{7}{u}^{7/2}-\\frac{4}{5}{u}^{5/2})|}_{2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}({0}^{7/2}-{2}^{7/2})-\\frac{4}{5}({0}^{5/2}-{2}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{2}^{9/2}}{7}+\\frac{{2}^{9/2}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{11/2}}{35} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{2}^{11}}}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
